{
  "_comment": "Per-package overrides for brew-changes' changelog resolution. `url` is a manual override for packages whose real changelog isn't reachable through GitHub auto-detection: most aren't GitHub-native (GNU/GNOME/language runtimes/closed-source apps), pointed at their own site or a self-hosted git. `repo` (\"owner/repo\") names the GitHub repo for packages that DO have one but whose Homebrew homepage, stable and head URLs don't reference GitHub, so auto-detection can't find it. `prerelease` shows only GitHub-flagged pre-releases, for preview channels (zed@preview) whose releases are interleaved with the stable channel's. `url`, `version` and `heading` are templates filled in from a version: `{version}` is the whole version, and `major`, `minor` and `patch` joined by `.`, `-` or `_` give its leading components (`{major.minor}` of 9.0.2 is 9.0, `{major-minor-patch}` of 1.3.1 is 1-3-1, a missing component is omitted). Any other `{...}` is literal text. `version` rewrites brew's version before matching headings and tags, for projects that document coarser versions (raycast's cask is 2.8.1.0, its changelog says v2.8: `{major.minor}`). `heading` describes a version heading when the default (Markdown ATX/Setext headings containing the version) can't find it or finds the wrong one: it must match the whole line, trimmed and without a leading `#` marker, and `{*}` matches any text (tmux's CHANGES: `CHANGES FROM {*} TO {version}`; vlc's NEWS, where `Changes between 3.0.24 and 3.0.25:` is 3.0.25's heading: `Changes between {*} and {version}:`). With a `heading` the section ends at the installed version's heading or, with nothing installed, the next heading for any version. HTML pages are converted to Markdown before matching. Verified with `cargo run --bin validate-changelogs -- 200` and `-- 200 -cask` as of 2026-10-16, which reports where each section ends; re-verify periodically since upstream projects reorganize docs. Keys are normally a bare formula/cask name, but formulae from a third-party tap (e.g. dart-lang/dart/dart) must use their tap-qualified name here, since that's what `brew outdated` reports as `homebrew::Package::name` and what `config::get` is called with — `get` does an exact match, no bare-name fallback.",
